# 🚀 PART 4: KUBERNETES DAEMONSET

## 1. What is a DaemonSet?

A **DaemonSet** is a Kubernetes workload controller that ensures a copy of a Pod runs on every eligible node, or on selected nodes, in a cluster.

In simple words:

**DaemonSet automatically runs one Pod on each eligible node.**

It is commonly used for node-level applications.

## 2. Why Do We Need DaemonSet?

Suppose a Kubernetes cluster contains 10 worker nodes.

You need a log collection application on every node.

Creating 10 Pods manually would be difficult to maintain.

DaemonSet automatically manages the Pods.

When a new eligible node joins the cluster, the DaemonSet creates a Pod on that node.

When a node is removed, the corresponding DaemonSet Pod is cleaned up.

## 3. DaemonSet Architecture

```text
             Kubernetes Cluster
                     |
                     v
                  DaemonSet
                     |
          -----------------------
          |          |          |
          v          v          v
        Node 1     Node 2     Node 3
          |          |          |
          v          v          v
        Pod        Pod        Pod
```

When a new eligible node is added:

```text
New Node Joins
      |
      v
DaemonSet Controller
      |
      v
Creates Pod on New Node
```

## 4. Real-World DaemonSet Use Cases

| Use Case | Example |
|---|---|
| Log collection | Fluent Bit, Fluentd |
| Monitoring | Prometheus Node Exporter |
| Security | Falco |
| Networking | Calico node agent |
| Node management | Node-level system agents |

### Example

A company uses Fluent Bit to collect container logs from all Kubernetes nodes.

Instead of deploying separate Pods manually, the company uses a DaemonSet.

This ensures every eligible node runs a log collection agent.

## 5. DaemonSet vs Deployment

| Deployment | DaemonSet |
|---|---|
| Maintains a desired number of replicas | Runs Pods on eligible nodes |
| Used for application workloads | Used for node-level services |
| Pods are scheduled according to available resources and constraints | Pods are associated with eligible nodes |
| Uses a replica count | Does not require a replica count |

**Important:** DaemonSet is not an old version of Deployment. Both are separate Kubernetes controllers with different purposes.

